A closure is a function that keeps access to the variables from the scope it was created in, even after that outer scope has finished running. Closures are less mysterious than people make them sound.

```javascript
function makeCounter() {
  let count = 0;
  return function () {
    count += 1;
    return count;
  };
}

const counter = makeCounter();
console.log(counter()); // 1
console.log(counter()); // 2
```

`makeCounter()` returns the inner function and then finishes. The inner function still holds a reference to `count`, so each call to `counter()` can read and update it. `count` isn't global and nothing else can touch it, so it stays private to that counter.

Each call to `makeCounter()` creates its own `count`, so `const other = makeCounter()` would start a separate count at 0.

